    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite a LinkedIn post of up to 500 words about a lesson learned from shipping a feature that nobody used.\n\nPlain prose only: no headers, no bullet points, no bold text, no em dashes. Write like a person.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nTwo years ago I spent about four months building a feature that, as far as I can tell, was used by nine people. Not nine thousand. Nine. And I think three of them were on my team testing it.\n\nThe feature was a customizable dashboard. Users could drag widgets around, pick which metrics showed up, save different layouts for different days. We had heard from a handful of customers that our default view didn't fit their workflow, and I took that and ran with it. I wrote a spec. I got design involved. We built a whole layout engine. I remember demoing it to the sales team and feeling genuinely proud of how smooth the drag and drop felt.\n\nThen we shipped it, and nothing happened. No spike in engagement. No support tickets, good or bad. A few people opened the customization panel, moved one widget, and never came back to it.\n\nFor a while I told myself it was a discoverability problem. We added a tooltip. We added a banner. We put it in the release notes twice. Nothing changed.\n\nWhat finally made it click was a call with one of the customers who had originally asked for it. I asked her why she hadn't set up her own layout. She said, and I'm paraphrasing only slightly, \"I don't want to design a dashboard. I want you to know what I need to see.\" She had never wanted flexibility. She wanted us to be right about the default. Flexibility was what she asked for because it was the easiest thing to say when the default was wrong.\n\nThat's the lesson I keep coming back to. When someone asks for a feature, they are usually describing a solution they can imagine, not the problem they actually have. My job was to dig underneath the request, and instead I took it at face value because it was concrete and buildable and I could picture the demo.\n\nThe honest version of the story is that I was also a little bored. The default view was a hard, unglamorous problem that required looking at a lot of data and talking to a lot of people and probably ending up somewhere unsatisfying. A layout engine was fun. I could see progress every day. I let that pull me.\n\nWhat I do differently now is pretty simple. Before I write a spec, I try to say the problem out loud without mentioning any solution, and I check whether the customer nods. If I can't do that, I'm not ready to build. And when a request comes in as a fully formed feature, I treat that as a signal that I haven't asked enough questions yet, not as a shortcut.\n\nWe eventually rebuilt the default view based on what people actually looked at first every morning. It took three weeks. Engagement went up noticeably. Nobody asked for customization again.\n\nI still think about those nine users sometimes. I hope the drag and drop was nice for them.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nWe spent four months building a custom dashboard builder. Six weeks after launch, 1.8% of accounts had created a dashboard. Half of those were internal test accounts.\n\nI still think about that number.\n\nThe feature wasn't badly built. The engineering was solid, the drag and drop worked, we shipped on time. It just turned out that almost nobody wanted to build a dashboard. They wanted to know one thing: whether the number they cared about was going up or down this week.\n\nHere's where I went wrong, and it wasn't a research failure in the way you might expect. We did talk to customers. Nineteen of them, over three weeks. A lot of them asked for better reporting. Some of them said the words \"custom dashboard\" out loud, unprompted. I took that as validation and moved on.\n\nWhat I didn't do was ask what they would stop doing once they had it. That question, which I now ask in every discovery conversation, would have saved us a quarter. Because the honest answer for most of those customers was nothing. They were already pulling a CSV into a spreadsheet once a month, it took twenty minutes, and it was fine. The pain was real but small, and small pain does not motivate anyone to learn a new tool.\n\nThe other thing I missed is that people are generous in interviews. When you ask someone what they want, they will help you. They will brainstorm with you. They will describe a feature because describing a feature feels more useful than saying \"honestly, this isn't a big deal for me.\" That generosity is easy to mistake for demand.\n\nNow I try to look for evidence that someone has already tried to solve the problem themselves. A spreadsheet held together with formulas nobody understands. A Zapier automation. A recurring calendar reminder. Somebody paying a contractor to do it manually. Those workarounds are expensive and awkward and they exist anyway, which tells you the pain is worth money. Nobody builds a workaround for a problem they don't have.\n\nWe eventually killed the dashboard builder and replaced it with a weekly email showing four metrics with an arrow next to each one. It took a sprint. Open rates sit around 60% and it comes up in sales calls constantly.\n\nThe lesson I keep relearning is that requests tell you where people are looking, not what they will do. The gap between those two things is where roadmaps go to die.\n\nIf you're about to commit a quarter to something, one question worth sitting with: what is the customer doing today instead, and how much does it actually cost them? If you can't answer that with specifics, you probably aren't ready to build yet.\n\nI wasn't. Four months taught me that better than any framework could have.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
